 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "In 1859, America and Britain almost went to war over a pig.",
   "s": 0.12,
   "e": 3.799
  },
  {
   "text": "On San Juan Island, both countries claimed the same land.",
   "s": 4.099,
   "e": 6.667
  },
  {
   "text": "Then a British pig wandered into an American's potato patch.",
   "s": 6.967,
   "e": 9.742
  },
  {
   "text": "So farmer Lyman Cutlar shot it.",
   "s": 10.042,
   "e": 11.337
  },
  {
   "text": "He offered ten dollars. The British demanded a hundred.",
   "s": 11.637,
   "e": 14.122
  },
  {
   "text": "So the U.S. Army sent 64 soldiers. Britain sent three warships.",
   "s": 14.422,
   "e": 18.137
  },
  {
   "text": "Then it got worse: 461 Americans dug in against 2,000 British troops.",
   "s": 18.437,
   "e": 23.157
  },
  {
   "text": "But the British admiral refused to fire.",
   "s": 23.457,
   "e": 25.16
  },
  {
   "text": "\"I will not involve two great nations in a war over a squabble about a pig.\"",
   "s": 25.46,
   "e": 29.185
  },
  {
   "text": "The standoff lasted 12 years. The only casualty? The pig.",
   "s": 29.365,
   "e": 32.221
  }
 ],
